(def keys-page "(keys map)\n\nReturns an iterator over borrows of a map's keys.\n\nA `sorted-map` yields them in key order, and the order of a `hash-map` is\nnot fixed. The map stays usable.\n\n(local prices (sorted-map \"pear\" 3 \"fig\" 1))\n(collect (map (keys prices) str))\n=> (vec (str \"fig\") (str \"pear\"))\n\n(len prices)\n=> 2")
(def update-page "(update value key function default)\n\nApplies a function to a field of a struct, or to the value at a map's key.\n\nWith a keyword key, `(update item :count neg)` returns the struct with the\nfield replaced by the function's result, and takes no default. The function\ntakes the field as `(:count item)` reads it, which borrows a field that is\nnot `copy`, so a function that owns its argument is an error. Rust itself\ncould move the field, and RFC 0059 leaves that open. The call moves the\nstruct's other fields. A base that reads fields from a name updates in\nplace, and a base rooted in a call runs once.\n\nWith any other key, the call applies the function to the value at the key\nand returns the map.\n\n`function` takes the value at `key`, or `default` when the key is missing,\nand the result is stored at `key`. `default` runs before the call, whether\nor not the call uses it. The call moves the map.\n\n`key` has the type of the map's keys: a string literal is a `(ref str)`, so\na `string` key takes `(str \"name\")`, and a `(ref str)` key takes\n`(as-str name)`.\n\n(local scores (update (sorted-map \"ana\" 1) \"ana\" (fn (n) (+ n 1)) 0))\n(get scores \"ana\")\n=> (some (ref 2))\n\n(local more (update scores \"bo\" (fn (n) (+ n 1)) 0))\n(get more \"bo\")\n=> (some (ref 1))")
(def into-page "(into-sorted-map pairs)\n\nBuilds a `sorted-map` from a vector of pairs.\n\n`pairs` is a `(vec (pair key value))`, and the call moves it. Keys implement\n`ord`, and the last pair of a repeated key wins.\n\n(local prices (into-sorted-map (vec (pair \"pear\" 3) (pair \"pear\" 5))))\n(get prices \"pear\")\n=> (some (ref 5))")
